  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A weak front and surface low lift slowly back to the \nnorth through tonight. The threat for flooding is greatest from now \nthrough the evening.\n\nA stationary front remains over the area this afternoon and is \npositioned from central to SE VA. A weak sfc low has begun to \ndevelop along the front. Meanwhile, an upper low is spinning over GA \nand a shortwave on the NE side of the low is moving north toward the \narea. As a result, numerous tstms have developed along the front \n(just south of the I-64 Corridor), which have prompted a few Flash \nFlood Warnings. Rain rates have been impressive (1.5\"-2\" in 30 mins \nas per gauges) given PWs on the order of 2-2.4\". The storms will \ngradually propagate to the SW along outflows through the evening, \nwith additional instances of flash flooding possible given the rain \nrates and weak deep layer flow. The low and attendant front are \nforecast to lift slowly northward through tonight. The front should \nmake it to the north of the CWA by sunrise on Wednesday. Tstms will \ngradually decrease in coverage this evening with the loss of daytime \nheating, but showers will remain possible through the night with the \nfront lifting north. The best precip chances shift to the Piedmont, \nnorthern neck, and eastern shore overnight. While the greatest \nthreat for flooding will be from now-8 PM, isolated flooding can't \nbe ruled out overnight. The weak winds aloft combined with \nwidespread clouds will limit shear/instability and the chance for \nsevere weather. Warm and humid overnight with lows in the upper 60s \nto low 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Convective coverage is expected to decrease \nWednesday and especially late week into the weekend with warmer \ntemperatures returning to the area.\n\nShowers and a few storms are possible Wednesday morning across the \nMD Eastern Shore. Then, the front and shortwave will move well to \nour north during the day. Upper heights also rise a bit as ridging \nover the Atlantic builds westward. Convective coverage on Wednesday \nwill be much less than we are seeing today, with isolated to perhaps \nscattered aftn/evening storms possible across much of the area. WPC \nhas maintained the Marginal ERO for much of the area. Precip \ncoverage is expected to further decrease late week into the weekend \nas the upper trough to the west lifts northward and away from the \narea and ridging continues to build westward. With lower shower and \nstorm coverage, temperatures return into the upper 80s and low 90s \nThursday through Sunday. Afternoon heat index values climb back into \nthe upper 90s and low 100s late week into the weekend. While \napparent temps may briefly creep into Heat Advisory range (105+) in \nthe afternoon, am not expecting the need for heat headlines at this \ntime.",
